Majjhima I: instruction in particular situations (@buddhist-mn-first-fifty: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-middle-length, #philosophy @buddhist-mn-middle-fifty, #buddhism @buddhist-mn-middle-fifty) { r_5WR24A6GZ13VJ05P4ZGCBQ6TZB
**Read:** MN 1–50 in [The Middle Length Discourses of the Buddha](../sources/Middle-length.lingua#r_7DP0G07DRGWNMD81GH20R1RTNB), completing this division in order. Read the general introduction with the first division and consult notes when an interpretation depends on them.

Attend to the audience and to how an instruction begins, develops, and answers the difficulty posed. Compare a practical instruction with its ethical assumptions and its account of the goal.

**Work through the text:** Choose one extended exchange. Separate the speaker’s commitments, the interlocutor’s concessions, and the conclusion. Compare it with an SN teaching you have already read.

**Check:** locate the passages another reader would need to challenge your reconstruction. Distinguish an example of teaching in context from a rule you have inferred for every possible audience.

**Continue:** [Majjhima II: disputes, reasons, and audiences](MN-Middle-fifty.lingua#r_3TQT24Z662C8NQ9VPS1ASDF201).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
